WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.539
Run those platforms separately
and you get split-brain DNS:

2
00:00:03.539 --> 00:00:08.025
the same name giving different answers
depending on where the query starts.

3
00:00:08.025 --> 00:00:09.638
A designed split is fine.

4
00:00:09.638 --> 00:00:14.087
The problem is unplanned drift
between systems with no shared state.

5
00:00:14.087 --> 00:00:15.675
Here's the classic case.

6
00:00:15.675 --> 00:00:18.414
A workload in AWS only
gets an Azure private

7
00:00:18.414 --> 00:00:21.837
endpoint's private address
through a forwarding rule.

8
00:00:21.837 --> 00:00:24.002
If that rule is missing or stale,

9
00:00:24.002 --> 00:00:27.499
the name still resolves,
just to the public address.

10
00:00:27.499 --> 00:00:30.674
Microsoft attributes
roughly seventy percent of

11
00:00:30.674 --> 00:00:34.075
private-endpoint access-denied
cases to exactly that.

12
00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:36.337
The same drift happens with SaaS paths,

13
00:00:36.337 --> 00:00:40.437
and with security blocks that only
apply on the resolver that answers.

14
00:00:40.437 --> 00:00:44.750
Either way, the failure is silent,
and it shows up as something else.

15
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.362
Changes come faster than any
manual process can track.

16
00:00:48.362 --> 00:00:52.387
Only a control plane that holds the
policy keeps everything in step.

17
00:00:52.387 --> 00:00:54.720
So what do the mandates actually require?
